\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Inertia along a regular path}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{On a fixed finite complex Hermitian space, let #{H(t)} be a norm-continuous path of invertible self-adjoint operators on a real interval. Its positive and negative dimensions are constant.}

\proof{\p{In dimension zero both dimensions vanish. Otherwise, at any parameter the smallest absolute spectral value is some #{c>0}. A perturbation of norm less than #{c} leaves the quadratic form positive on the old positive subspace and negative on the old negative subspace. A positive subspace projects injectively onto the new positive spectral subspace, since its intersection with the new nonpositive subspace is zero. The corresponding negative statement also holds. The two old dimensions already exhaust the space, so each new dimension equals the old one. The dimensions are locally constant and the interval is connected.}}
